{"diagnosis": "Lock inversion between queue_lock and stats_lock due to opposite acquisition order in worker and monitor threads under forced overlap via barrier.", "root_cause": "Worker acquires queue_lock then stats_lock; monitor acquires stats_lock then queue_lock. When both hold their first lock and synchronize at the barrier, each waits for the other's lock, causing deadlock.", "confidence": 0.95, "reasoning_summary": "Deadlock occurs because worker and monitor acquire two mutexes in opposite order when force_overlap is true, and the barrier ensures they hold the first lock while waiting for the second.", "expected_effect": "Eliminates lock inversion by ensuring both functions acquire locks in the same order (queue_lock before stats_lock) even during overlap.", "risk_notes": ["Changing lock order in queue_inspect may affect monitoring semantics if stats are accessed inconsistently with queue state, but since inspect only reads outstanding counts and does not modify state, and queue_lock protects the same data as stats_lock in this context, the change is safe."], "edits": [{"file": "src/queue.c", "search": "int queue_inspect(JobQueue *q, int force_overlap) {
    /* Intentional fault: monitoring acquires the same locks in reverse order. */
    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->stats_lock);
    if (force_overlap) pthread_barrier_wait(&q->reproduce_barrier);
    if (force_overlap) fprintf(stderr, "MONITOR HOLD stats WAIT queue\n");
    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->queue_lock);
    int outstanding = q->length - q->completed;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->queue_lock);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->stats_lock);
    return outstanding;
}", "replacement": "int queue_inspect(JobQueue *q, int force_overlap) {
